1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,110
Dancing is something that humans do when they want to have a good time.

2
00:00:06,110 --> 00:00:10,890
It's a universal response to music, found in all cultures.

3
00:00:10,890 --> 00:00:17,722
But what's only been discovered recently is that dancing not only makes us feel good,

4
00:00:17,722 --> 00:00:20,910
it's also extremely good for our health.

5
00:00:20,910 --> 00:00:24,177
Dancing, like other forms of exercise,

6
00:00:24,177 --> 00:00:30,710
releases hormones, such as dopamine, which make us feel relaxed and happy.

7
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:35,580
And it also reduces feelings of stress or anxiety.

8
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:43,090
Dancing is also a sociable activity, which is another reason it makes us feel good.

9
00:00:43,090 --> 00:00:49,008
One study compared people's enjoyment of dancing at home in front of a video

10
00:00:49,008 --> 00:00:52,390
with dancing in a group in a studio.

11
00:00:52,390 --> 00:00:56,940
The people dancing in a group reported feeling happier

12
00:00:57,280 --> 00:00:59,880
whereas those dancing alone did not.

13
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:05,460
In another experiment, university researchers at York

14
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:06,349
and Sheffield took a group of students

15
00:01:06,349 --> 00:01:13,280
and sent each of them into a lab where music was played for five minutes.

16
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:20,555
Each had to choose from three options: to sit and listen quietly to the music,

17
00:01:20,555 --> 00:01:27,830
to cycle on an exercise bike while they listened, or to get up and dance.

18
00:01:27,830 --> 00:01:33,890
All were given cognitive tasks to perform before and after.

19
00:01:33,890 --> 00:01:36,140
The result showed that those

20
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:43,630
who chose to dance showed much more creativity when doing problem-solving tasks.

21
00:01:43,630 --> 00:01:47,720
Doctor Lovatt at the University of Hertfordshire

22
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:54,900
believes dance could be a very useful way to help people suffering from mental health problems.

23
00:01:54,900 --> 00:02:05,510
He thinks dance should be prescribed as therapy to help people overcome issues such as depression.

24
00:02:05,510 --> 00:02:13,906
It's well established that dance is a good way of encouraging adolescent girls to take exercise

25
00:02:13,906 --> 00:02:16,530
but what about older people?

26
00:02:16,530 --> 00:02:22,750
Studies have shown that there are enormous benefits for people in their sixties and beyond.

27
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:29,090
One of the great things about dance is that there are no barriers to participation.

28
00:02:29,090 --> 00:02:35,260
Anyone can have a go, even those whose standard of fitness is quite low.

29
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:40,440
Dance can be especially beneficial for older adults

30
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:43,003
who can't run or do more intense workouts,

31
00:02:43,003 --> 00:02:46,390
or for those who don't want to.

32
00:02:46,390 --> 00:02:55,580
One 2015 study found that even a gentle dance workout helps to promote a healthy heart.

33
00:02:55,580 --> 00:03:01,973
And there's plenty of evidence which suggests that dancing lowers the risk of falls,

34
00:03:01,973 --> 00:03:05,640
which could result in a broken hip, for example

35
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:09,280
by helping people to improve their balance.

36
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:15,430
There are some less obvious benefits of dance for older people too.

37
00:03:15,430 --> 00:03:24,400
One thing I hadn't realised before researching this topic was that dance isn't just a physical challenge.

38
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:27,480
It also requires a lot of concentration

39
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:31,340
because you need to remember different steps and routines.

40
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:36,420
For older people, this kind of activity is especially important

41
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:41,840
because it forces their brain to process things more quickly

42
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:44,040
and to retain more information.

43
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:51,516
Current research also shows that dance promotes a general sense of well-being in older participants,

44
00:03:51,516 --> 00:03:56,500
which can last up to a week after a class.

45
00:03:56,500 --> 00:04:00,380
Participants report feeling less tired

46
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:02,893
and having greater motivation to be more active

47
00:04:02,893 --> 00:04:10,270
and do daily activities such as gardening or walking to the shops or a park.

48
00:04:10,270 --> 00:04:16,820
Ballroom or country dancing, both popular with older people

49
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:19,060
have to be done in groups.

50
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:24,160
They require collaboration and often involve touching a dance partner,

51
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:28,750
all of which encourages interaction on the dance floor.

52
00:04:28,750 --> 00:04:31,720
This helps to develop new relationships

53
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:36,138
and can reduce older people's sense of isolation,

54
00:04:36,138 --> 00:04:40,360
which is a huge problem in many countries.

55
00:04:40,360 --> 00:04:43,500
I also looked at the benefits of Zumba.

56
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:50,929
Fifteen million people in 180 countries now regularly take a Zumba class,

57
00:04:50,929 --> 00:04:56,500
an aerobic workout based on Latin American dance moves.

58
00:04:56,500 --> 00:04:59,780
John Porcari, a professor of exercise

59
00:04:59,780 --> 00:05:03,480
and sport science at the University of Wisconsin,

60
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:05,973
analysed a group of women

61
00:05:05,973 --> 00:05:13,950
who were Zumba regulars and found that a class lasting 40 minutes burns about 370 calories.

62
00:05:13,950 --> 00:05:22,360
This is similar to moderately intense exercises like step aerobics or kickboxing.

63
00:05:22,360 --> 00:05:28,397
A study in the American Journal of Health Behavior showed that when women

64
00:05:28,397 --> 00:05:33,505
with obesity did Zumba three times a week for 16 weeks,

65
00:05:33,505 --> 00:05:41,400
they lost an average of 1.2 kilos and lowered their percentage of body fat by 1%.

66
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:48,759
More importantly, the women enjoyed the class so much that they made it a habit

67
00:05:48,759 --> 00:05:52,640
and continued to attend classes at least once a week

68
00:05:53,080 --> 00:05:57,100
very unusual for an aerobic exercise programme.

69
00:05:57,100 --> 00:06:02,880
Dance is never going to compete with high-intensity workouts

70
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:04,582
when it comes to physical fitness gains,

71
00:06:04,582 --> 00:06:09,680
but its popularity is likely to keep on rising

72
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:13,000
because it's such a fun way to keep fit.
